# Line-ending policy for the whole repo.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` makes Git store every text file with LF in the
# repository AND check it out with LF in the working tree on *every*
# platform — Linux and Windows alike. Because .gitattributes takes
# precedence over each machine's `core.autocrlf` / `core.eol`, this
# settles the policy in the repo instead of per-developer config, which
# is what eliminates the "LF will be replaced by CRLF" warnings.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binaries — never normalize, never diff as text. `text=auto` already
# detects common binaries (images, archives, libraries) by content, so
# only the formats this project actually carries or handles are pinned:
# the bundled report font, and the vector / columnar dataset formats the
# workloads read, whose raw numeric payloads shouldn't be left to a
# content sniff.
*.ttf     binary
*.fvec    binary
*.fvecs   binary
*.ivec    binary
*.ivecs   binary
*.bvec    binary
*.bvecs   binary
*.dvec    binary
*.dvecs   binary
*.parquet binary

# (No .bat/.cmd/.ps1 are tracked. If Windows-only batch scripts are ever
# added and misbehave with LF, pin them here, e.g.:
#   *.bat text eol=crlf
#   *.cmd text eol=crlf
# )
